{
  "dom_id": "step-q83475",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14228735/assets/856b131ba85b8fd1da0050ead917d5757b4b4eea46aca54bef7d7b103a896bc6.png)\n\nFind the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+1$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\le 0,\\,y\\ge 0,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4\\},$ shown above.",
  "worked_solution": "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve  ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x,\\,-2y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x=0 \\\\ -2y=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0).$ Since the point $(0,0)$ does not belong to the interior of $D,$ we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(0,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,0).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =(t)^{2}-(0)^{2}+1 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (-2,0),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{2}+1)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 2t & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=0$ is not inside the interval $t\\in (-2,0).$\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨0,t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(0,t) \\\\  & =(0)^{2}-(t)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1-{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (1-{t}^{2})^{′} & =0 \\\\ -2t & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=0$ is not inside the interval $t\\in (0,2).$\n\n- The quarter-circle connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨t,\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}-(\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}})^{2}+1 \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (-2,0),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (2{t}^{2}-3)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 4t & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution, $t=0$ is not inside the interval $t\\in (-2,0).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, we obtain three candidates on the boundary:\n\n$$\n(-2,0),\\,(0,0),\\,(0,2)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $5$ | $-3$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-3$ at the points $(0,2),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $5$ at the point $(-2,0).$",
